.TH LOGANALYZER 1 "November 2026" "Custom Shell" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
loganalyzer \- count lines, words and log levels in a log file using mmap()
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loganalyzer
[\fB\-k\fR \fIkeyword\fR] [\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-h\fR] \fIlogfile\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B loganalyzer
maps the whole log file into memory with
.BR mmap (2)
and scans it once, line by line. It reports the total lines, empty
lines, words and the length of the longest line.
.PP
The command is part of the Custom Shell. Its entry point is
.BR loganalyzer_main(argc,\ argv) ,
which the shell calls when the user types
.BR loganalyzer .
It must be built on Linux (or WSL), because
.I sys/mman.h
is not available on Windows.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-k " keyword"
Count the lines that contain \fIkeyword\fR (case-sensitive).
.TP
.B \-t
Count lines by log level: ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG. Only the first
level found in a line is counted.
.TP
.B \-v
Also print the file size in bytes.
.TP
.B \-h
Print help and exit.
.SH SIGNALS
Pressing Ctrl+C (SIGINT) during a scan stops it and prints
.B [Interrupted by Ctrl+C - partial results]
followed by the counts so far. Ctrl+C goes back to its normal behavior
when the command finishes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including an empty file.
.TP
.B 1
Error: bad option, missing \fB\-k\fR word, no file given, or the file
could not be opened.
.TP
.B 130
Stopped by Ctrl+C.
.SH BUILDING FOR TESTING
loganalyzer.c has no main() of its own. To test it alone, use a
throwaway main:
.PP
.nf
.RS
echo 'int loganalyzer_main(int, char **); int main(int c, char **v) { return loganalyzer_main(c, v); }' > main.c
gcc \-Wall \-o loganalyzer loganalyzer.c main.c
.RE
.fi
.SH TEST FILES
.TP
.B sample.log
12 lines (line 6 is empty), 551 bytes, each line ending in a newline:
.PP
.nf
.RS
2026-11-01 10:00:01 INFO Server started on port 8080
2026-11-01 10:00:05 DEBUG Loading config file /etc/app.conf
2026-11-01 10:01:12 INFO User alice logged in
2026-11-01 10:02:30 WARN Disk usage at 85%
2026-11-01 10:03:44 ERROR Database connection timeout

2026-11-01 10:04:10 INFO Retrying database connection
2026-11-01 10:04:15 ERROR Database connection timeout
2026-11-01 10:05:00 INFO User bob logged in
2026-11-01 10:06:21 WARN Slow response: 2300 ms
2026-11-01 10:07:02 DEBUG Cache hit ratio 0.92
2026-11-01 10:08:59 INFO User alice logged out
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B empty.log
An empty file, made with: \fBtouch empty.log\fR
.TP
.B big.log
1,000,000 lines (about 34 MB), made with:
.PP
.nf
.RS
seq 1 1000000 | sed 's/.*/2026-11-01 INFO request & ok/' > big.log
.RE
.fi
.SH TESTS AND EXPECTED OUTPUT
.SS 1. Basic stats (exit 0)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer sample.log
===== loganalyzer report: sample.log =====
Total lines      : 12
Empty lines      : 1
Total words      : 75
Longest line     : 59 chars
.RE
.fi
.SS 2. Log levels (exit 0)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-t sample.log
===== loganalyzer report: sample.log =====
Total lines      : 12
Empty lines      : 1
Total words      : 75
Longest line     : 59 chars
\-\-\- Log levels \-\-\-
  ERROR  : 2
  WARN   : 2
  INFO   : 5
  DEBUG  : 2
.RE
.fi
.SS 3. Keyword (exit 0)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-k timeout sample.log
===== loganalyzer report: sample.log =====
Total lines      : 12
Empty lines      : 1
Total words      : 75
Longest line     : 59 chars
Lines with "timeout" : 2
.RE
.fi
.SS 4. All options together (exit 0)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-t \-k alice \-v sample.log
===== loganalyzer report: sample.log =====
Total lines      : 12
Empty lines      : 1
Total words      : 75
Longest line     : 59 chars
Lines with "alice" : 2
\-\-\- Log levels \-\-\-
  ERROR  : 2
  WARN   : 2
  INFO   : 5
  DEBUG  : 2
File size        : 551 bytes
.RE
.fi
.SS 5. Help (exit 0)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-h
Usage: loganalyzer [\-k keyword] [\-t] [\-v] [\-h] <logfile>
  \-k keyword  count lines containing keyword
  \-t          count log levels (ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG)
  \-v          verbose (show file size)
  \-h          show this help
.RE
.fi
.SS 6. Empty file (exit 0)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer empty.log
File is empty.
.RE
.fi
.SS 7. File does not exist (exit 1)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer missing.log
Error opening log file: No such file or directory
.RE
.fi
.SS 8. No file given (exit 1)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer
Error: no log file given
.RE
.fi
Then prints the help text from test 5.
.SS 9. Bad option (exit 1)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-z sample.log
loganalyzer: invalid option \-\- 'z'
.RE
.fi
Then prints the help text.
.SS 10. \-k without a word (exit 1)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-k
loganalyzer: option requires an argument \-\- 'k'
.RE
.fi
Then prints the help text.
.SS 11. Big file, mmap speed test (exit 0)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-t \-v big.log
===== loganalyzer report: big.log =====
Total lines      : 1000000
Empty lines      : 0
Total words      : 5000000
Longest line     : 34 chars
\-\-\- Log levels \-\-\-
  ERROR  : 0
  WARN   : 0
  INFO   : 1000000
  DEBUG  : 0
File size        : 33888896 bytes
.RE
.fi
.SS 12. Ctrl+C (exit 130)
Run test 11 and press Ctrl+C during the scan. The scan is fast, so a
bigger file may be needed to catch it. The command prints the partial
results described under SIGNALS.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mmap (2),
.BR munmap (2),
.BR open (2),
.BR fstat (2),
.BR getopt (3),
.BR signal (2)
